==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|de|Phylogenie}}, coined by Ernst Haeckel in 1866, a neologism created as if borrowed from a Classic Greek word {{l|grc|φυλογένεια}}, composed from {{der|en|grc|φῦλον||tribe, genus, species}} + {{der|en|grc|-γένεια||-geny (generation, production)}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/faɪˈlɒd͡ʒəni/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/faɪˈlɑd͡ʒəni/|a=US,Canada}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Neøn-phylogeny.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|systematics}} The [[evolutionary]] [[history]] of groups of [[organism]]s, such as species or [[clade]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=[[w:Paul J. McAuley|Paul McAuley]]|title=Beyond the Burn Line|publisher=Gollancz|page=43|passage=It was a bestiary more than a hundred years old, its entries organised by a quaintly outdated '''phylogeny''' and illustrated with hand-tinted plates.}}
# {{lb|en|systematics|informal}} A [[phylogenetic]] [[diagram]].
#: {{hypo|en|tree of life<id:metaphor for phylogenetic relationships; diagram thereof>}}<!--a type of such diagram-->
#: {{hol|en|tree of life<id:metaphor for phylogenetic relationships; diagram thereof>}}<!--diagram of the entire tree, of which other diagrams are portions-->
# The [[historical]] [[development]] of a human social or racial group.
#: {{ux|en|Understanding the '''phylogeny''' of this musical group helps us understand its music.}}
# The historical development of any thing, idea, etc.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|title=The Journey of Child Development|isbn=0203856856
|passage=Indeed, in a recent review article, Mithen (2009) traces the '''phylogeny''' of human communication {{...}}}}

